OK md:1079 corpus/instruments/capture-binding-check.py:1-92  [#!/usr/bin/env python3 ⏎ """Bind every capture-manifest record to its registry row and to fetch provenance. ⏎  ⏎ Usage: python3 corpus/instruments/capture-binding-check.py [--root DIR] [--selftest] [--scratch DIR] ⏎  ⏎ The round-33 C1 counter, hardened in round 34. A capture directory can hold real ⏎ bytes and still be unauditable: nothing in `manifest.json` says which documented ⏎ URL those bytes came from, when they were fetched, or whether a file in the ⏎ directory is accounted for at all. This instrument walks every snapshot ⏎ directory under `corpus/source-snapshots/` - recursively - and applies four ⏎ rules. ⏎  ⏎ A. MANIFEST PRESENCE AND PER-FILE BINDING. Every snapshot directory - a ⏎ directory that holds a `manifest.json` or that holds any captured source ⏎ file of its own - must carry a `manifest.json`; a directory with captures ⏎ and no manifest is `FAIL rule=missing-manifest dir=<dir>`. Inside a ⏎ manifest directory, every capture file must be accounted for: ⏎  ⏎ A1. it is named by the `path` of at least one `sources[]` record; or ⏎ A2. the manifest's top-level `orphans` list declares it, as an object ⏎ `{"path": "<file name in this directory>", "reason": "<non-empty>"}`. ⏎ A declared orphan keeps the exit status at 0 and is still printed as ⏎ `orphan=<file>` on the AUDIT line - the listing is the record that ⏎ the file is known and unbound, not a silent pass; ⏎ otherwise `FAIL rule=orphan-capture file=<file> dir=<dir>`. ⏎ A declaration whose file is absent is `FAIL rule=orphan-declared-missing` ⏎ (a stale listing), and a malformed `orphans` list is a manifest-shape ⏎ error (exit 2). Orphans never change `records=`/`bound=`/`unbound=`, so a ⏎ declaration cannot be used to inflate the binding counts. ⏎  ⏎ Nested snapshot directories are audited in their own right: the walk ⏎ descends whether or not the parent is a snapshot directory, and a nested ⏎ directory is named by its path below `corpus/source-snapshots/` ⏎ (`round-30/inner`). A parent's per-file check considers only its own direct ⏎ files, because a nested directory's files are that directory's subject. ⏎  ⏎ B. URL BINDING (claims-erratum-1, the L1's id-scoped ruling). Every manifest ⏎ record must bind to its OWN `corpus/SOURCES.md` registry row: ⏎  ⏎ B1. the row `| <record id> | ... |` must exist - otherwise ⏎ `FAIL rule=no-row record=<id>` (a fabricated id); ⏎ B2. the row id must be unique in the registry - two rows with the same ⏎ id are `FAIL rule=dup-id record=<id> rows=<n>`; ⏎ B3. a row whose `status` cell is `rejected` cannot anchor a record: ⏎ `FAIL rule=rejected-row-anchor record=<id> row=<id>`. A rejected row ⏎ is a documented refusal, so a capture claiming it is bound to a ⏎ source the lane has refused; ⏎ B4. the row's source cell must carry exactly one `https://` token. A row ⏎ with two or more is `FAIL rule=double-https-row row=<id> n=<n>` and ⏎ cannot unambiguously bind anything - the record pointing at it is ⏎ `FAIL rule=url-ambiguous record=<id> row=<id> tokens=<n>`; ⏎ B5. the record's `url` must equal, byte-for-byte, the `https://` token in ⏎ that row's source cell (column 2) - otherwise ⏎ `FAIL rule=url-mismatch record=<id>`. A record with no `url` field at ⏎ all is `url-mismatch ... detail=absent`. ⏎  ⏎ A URL that also appears in a DIFFERENT row (the registry's S024/S029 and ⏎ S006/S028 duplicate-URL rows) or in prose outside the registry table is ⏎ NOT a failure: see the informational `multi_row_url=N` count on the AUDIT ⏎ line. The frozen claim's predicate (erratum 1) keeps its `unbound=0` ⏎ target under this reading, and the registry's pre-existing W04 ⏎ duplicate-URL warnings stay census facts outside this claim. ⏎  ⏎ C. PROVENANCE. Every record must carry either a strict-UTC `fetched_utc` or ⏎ `"archival": true`. A `fetched_utc` value is strict UTC only when it is an ⏎ ISO-8601 timestamp whose suffix is `Z` or `+00:00` and whose fields are a ⏎ real calendar instant, e.g. `2026-10-26T04:16:06Z`: ⏎  ⏎ - a placeholder (`pending`, `tbd`, `unknown`, `none`, ...) is ⏎ `FAIL rule=fetched-utc ... detail=pending`; ⏎ - a local or non-zero offset (`2026-10-25T23:14:00-03:00`, the round-32 ⏎ R8 field) is `FAIL rule=fetched-utc ... detail=local-offset`; ⏎ - anything else unparsable (`2026-13-01T00:00:00Z`, a bare date) is ⏎ `FAIL rule=fetched-utc ... detail=malformed`; ⏎ - an absent value is `FAIL rule=provenance-missing` when the record has ⏎ no archival exemption, `detail=absent` folded into that line. ⏎  ⏎ Records whose snapshot directory is `round-32` or a later round MUST carry ⏎ a strict-UTC `fetched_utc`: the archival exemption is closed for them, ⏎ because their fetch instant is recorded. A directory name that does not ⏎ parse as `round-<N>` is treated as a modern round (no archival exemption). ⏎ A present-but-invalid `fetched_utc` fails in every round, including the ⏎ legacy rounds - the field is an assertion whether or not the round requires ⏎ it. ⏎  ⏎ D. MANIFEST INTEGRITY. Duplicate records inside one manifest fail: two ⏎ records in the same directory carrying the same `id` is ⏎ `FAIL rule=dup-record field=id value=<id> count=<n>`, and two records ⏎ naming the same capture `path` is the `field=path` form. A duplicated ⏎ record means the manifest's record count overstates the captures it ⏎ documents. ⏎ ]
